# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS. Without it, `npm ci` FAILS on a clean checkout:
#
#     npm error code ERESOLVE
#     npm error While resolving: react-day-picker@8.10.1
#     npm error Found: react@19.2.8
#     npm error   peer react@"^16.8.0 || ^17.0.0 || ^18.0.0" from react-day-picker@8.10.1
#
# The React 18 → 19 upgrade on 2026-08-15 left react-day-picker at v8, whose
# peer range stops at React 18. Nothing on a developer machine notices, because
# node_modules is already populated — typecheck, tests and build all stay green.
# EVERYTHING THAT STARTS FROM A CLEAN CHECKOUT BREAKS:
#
#   • Cloudflare Pages, which builds and serves www.50mmretina.com. The website
#     silently stopped updating; the owner found it before I did.
#   • Typecheck and Security in GitHub Actions, red on every push since.
#   • The Android build, which also begins with `npm ci`.
#
# WHY THIS AND NOT AN `overrides` BLOCK. An overrides entry also fixes the
# install, but MEASURED: adding one makes npm reclassify 141 packages from dev
# to production in the lockfile. `.github/workflows/security.yml` runs
# `npm audit --omit=dev --audit-level=critical`, so that would quietly change
# what the security gate is even looking at. This file changes the resolver and
# nothing else — package.json and package-lock.json are untouched, and the same
# 1,010 packages install.
#
# THIS IS A HOLD, NOT THE CURE. The cure is react-day-picker v9, which supports
# React 19 properly; it renames props on the Calendar component, so it is a
# small migration with a visible UI to photograph first. Until then this keeps
# the website deployable, and the census in
# src/__tests__/cleanInstallResolves.test.ts names every other package whose
# React peer range would break the same way.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
legacy-peer-deps=true
